================================================================================
Data sets — published by their operators without licence terms
================================================================================

The following IP-range files are published by search-engine and AI-crawler operators to
let site owners verify their crawlers. They carry no stated licence. This product FETCHES
them at runtime and does not redistribute or vendor them. If you fork this project, keep
it that way.

  Google      https://developers.google.com/static/search/apis/ipranges/common-crawlers.json
              (and special-crawlers.json, user-triggered-fetchers.json)
  Bing        https://www.bing.com/toolbox/bingbot.json
  OpenAI      https://openai.com/gptbot.json, searchbot.json, chatgpt-user.json
  Anthropic   https://www.anthropic.com/anthropic-bots.json
  Apple       https://search.developer.apple.com/applebot.json
  Amazon      https://developer.amazon.com/amazonbot.json
  Perplexity  https://www.perplexity.com/perplexitybot.json

Cloud-provider public IP ranges (AWS, Google Cloud, Microsoft Azure Service Tags,
Cloudflare, DigitalOcean, Hetzner, OVH, Linode, Oracle Cloud, Fastly) are likewise fetched
at runtime from each provider's published endpoint.


================================================================================
Not bundled
================================================================================

  MaxMind GeoLite2 is deliberately NOT used. Its End User Licence Agreement requires
  licensees to destroy prior copies within 30 days of an update and forbids disclosure of
  the database to third parties without prior written consent, which is incompatible with
  a self-hostable open-source artifact. DB-IP Lite is used instead.

  Matomo device-detector data (LGPL-3.0), and the ua-parser regex set it is often paired
  with, are not used. Working out whether a visit came from a phone, a tablet or a computer
  is done in this repository's own code, from the low-entropy hints a browser volunteers and
  from an ordered set of plain substring tests over a curated catalogue. Nothing about that
  problem was worth a copyleft data file or a pattern set that runs against attacker-supplied
  text.
